latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Input Validation:\n   - Accept the input grid.\n   - Verify that the grid is valid (correct dimensions, valid color values).\n\n2. Brown Cell Identification:\n   - Scan the entire grid to identify all brown (9) cells.\n   - If no brown cells are found, return the input grid unchanged.\n\n3. Initialization:\n   - Create a copy of the input grid to modify.\n   - Create a set or list to keep track of brown cell coordinates.\n\n4. Transformation Loop:\n   - While there are still brown cells to process:\n     a. For each brown cell:\n        - Check its adjacent cells (up, down, left, right).\n        - If any adjacent cell is sky blue (8), mark this cell for replacement with sky blue.\n        - If no adjacent sky blue, find the first non-brown color among adjacent cells.\n        - If a replacement color is found, add this cell and its new color to a list of updates.\n     b. Apply all the updates from this iteration:\n        - Change the colors of the marked cells in the grid copy.\n        - Remove the updated cells from the set/list of brown cells.\n\n5. Finalization:\n   - Once all brown cells have been replaced, return the modified grid.\n\nThis plan addresses all the key points we identified in our reflection:\n- It handles cases with no brown cells by returning the input unchanged.\n- It prioritizes replacing brown with adjacent sky blue.\n- It falls back to the nearest non-brown color when sky blue isn't adjacent.\n- It continues until all brown cells are replaced.\n\nThe loop structure ensures that the process is iterative, allowing for cascading effects where newly placed sky blue cells can influence the replacement of other brown cells in subsequent iterations."
